            "latencyMs": 27728,
            "replyText": "A Docker image is a read-only template that contains all the instructions and files needed to run a specific application or service. It is like a blueprint or a recipe that defines what software, dependencies, and configurations are required to run a program. Images are immutable and can be shared across different systems.\n\nA container, on the other hand, is a running instance of a Docker image. It is a lightweight, executable, and isolated environment that runs the application defined by the image. Containers provide the actual runtime environment, including the operating system, libraries, and configurations, and allow the application to execute in isolation from the host system and other containers.\n\nIn short:\n- An image is a static file used to create containers.\n- A container is a dynamic, running instance of an image.\n\nFor example, if you have an image named \"nginx:1.27\", you can use it to create one or more containers. Each container runs the nginx service, but they are independent and can be managed separately (e.g., started, stopped, or scaled).",
            "recordedAt": "2026-09-27T22:55:58.877Z",
            "promptfooEvalId": "eval-ugn-2026-09-27T22:55:30"
        },
        {
            "caseId": "question-about-stopping-needs-no-tool",
            "passed": true,
            "reasons": [
                "called no tool, as expected"
            ],
            "toolCalls": [],
            "stopReason": "answered",
            "modelCalls": 1,
            "peakPromptTokens": 2996,
            "latencyMs": 16600,
            "replyText": "When you stop a container using `stop_container`, the data inside it—such as files, databases, or application state—is **preserved** as long as the container was created with persistent storage (e.g., volumes or bind mounts).\n\nHere’s what happens in detail:\n\n- **If the container uses volumes or bind mounts**: The data stored in those volumes is saved to the host machine and remains accessible after the container stops. This is the standard way to ensure data persistence.\n- **If the container uses only ephemeral storage (no volumes)**: The data is lost when the container stops, because it's stored only in memory or in temporary filesystems that are not persisted.\n\n> ⚠️ **Important**: Stopping a container does **not** delete or overwrite data. It only halts the process. To permanently remove data, you must explicitly delete the container or the associated volumes.\n\nTo verify whether a container has persistent storage, you can check its configuration using `get_container`, which shows mounts and volumes.\n\nFor example:\n```json\n\"mounts\": [\n  {\n    \"type\": \"bind\",\n    \"source\": \"/host/path\",\n    \"destination\": \"/container/path\"\n  }\n]\n```\nIf such a mount exists, data will survive the stop.\n\nIf you're unsure, ask me to inspect a specific container's configuration.",
            "recordedAt": "2026-09-27T22:29:49.122Z",
            "promptfooEvalId": "eval-EDt-2026-09-27T22:27:55"
